.SH DESCRIPTION
The _printf function is a simplified, custom implementation of the standard 
C library printf function. It processes a format string containing ordinary 
characters and conversion specifiers, and prints the corresponding output to 
the standard output (stdout).

Ordinary characters in the format string are written directly to the output. 
When a percent sign (%) is encountered, the function interprets the next 
character as a conversion specifier and handles it accordingly. The function 
retrieves the corresponding argument from the variadic argument list and 
prints it using helper functions.

The following conversion specifiers are supported:

.TP
.B %d, %i
Print a signed decimal integer.

.TP
.B %c
Print a single character.

.TP
.B %s
Print a null-terminated string.

.TP
.B %%
Print a literal percent sign.

If the format string is NULL, or if a percent sign appears at the end of the 
format string without a valid specifier, the function returns -1. Otherwise, 
it returns the total number of characters printed.





.SH EXAMPLES
1. Printing a string:
.br
.B _printf("Hello, %s!\\n", "Everyone!");
.br
Output:
.br
Hello, Everyone!

.PP
2. Printing an integer:
.br
.B _printf("Score: %d\\n", 98);
.br
Output:
.br
Score: 98

.PP
3. Printing a character:
.br
.B _printf("Grade: %c\\n", 'A');
.br
Output:
.br
Grade: A

.PP
4. Printing a percent sign:
.br
.B _printf("Completion: 100%%\\n");
.br
Output:
.br
Completion: 100%

.PP
5. Mixing different types:
.br
.B _printf("Name: %s, Age: %d, Initial: %c\\n", "Reem", 21, 'R');
.br
Output:
.br
Name: Reem, Age: 21, Initial: R

